const L3={title:'Consent, capacity and informed refusal',hook:'A patient signs a procedure form but cannot explain what will happen. Another refuses a recommended treatment after explaining the risks clearly. A signature and agreement are not substitutes for understanding and a valid decision process.',objectives:['Describe informed consent as a process rather than a form.','Differentiate decision-making capacity from a legal status determination.','Apply disclosure, understanding, voluntariness and authorization.','Analyze refusal, surrogate decisions and urgent exceptions with appropriate limits.'],sections:[
['Consent is specific and informed',[
'Consent authorizes a particular intervention under understood circumstances. Informed consent requires an appropriate discussion of the condition, proposed intervention, expected benefits, material risks and reasonable alternatives, including not proceeding. The details required by law depend on jurisdiction and context. The educational principle is stable: a person needs information relevant to the choice, an opportunity to ask questions and a voluntary decision. A generic admission form does not automatically resolve every later procedure decision.',
'The signed form documents part of the process; it does not prove that the process was adequate. Conversely, some valid consent interactions may be documented in ways other than a particular form, depending on the intervention and applicable requirements. Express consent is communicated directly, while implied consent can be inferred from conduct in an appropriate context. Extending an arm for a routine blood-pressure measurement is not unlimited permission for unrelated treatment. Scope follows the particular interaction.']],
['Assess capacity for the decision at hand',[
'Decision-making capacity is a clinical assessment of abilities relevant to a particular decision. Common elements include understanding information, appreciating its relevance to one’s situation, reasoning about options and communicating a choice. Capacity can vary with the complexity of the decision and over time. Pain, delirium, medication effects or communication barriers may impair participation temporarily. A diagnosis, disability, age or disagreement with the clinician does not alone establish incapacity.',
'Legal terminology such as competence can refer to a court’s determination, but usage and procedures vary. Avoid treating the words as a universal diagnostic shortcut. Support the person’s decision-making before concluding they cannot participate: provide language assistance, accessible explanations, hearing aids or a quieter setting as appropriate. If a reversible condition can be addressed and the decision can safely wait, reassessment may allow the patient to decide personally. Document the observed abilities and reasoning rather than only a label.',match('Identify the consent element',[['Explain options, risks and benefits','Disclosure'],['Patient describes the choice in their own words','Understanding'],['Decision is free of coercion','Voluntariness'],['Patient agrees to the particular intervention','Authorization']])]],
['Understanding and voluntariness need active attention',[
'Use plain language and manageable information segments. Ask the patient to explain what they understand about the proposed intervention and alternatives. This is a check on communication, not a school examination. Correct misunderstandings without shaming. A professional should not assume that a patient understood because they nodded, used a familiar medical term or signed quickly. The patient may be embarrassed to ask questions or may interpret a word differently from the team.',
'Voluntariness can be threatened by coercion, manipulation or undue pressure. A strong recommendation is not automatically coercion, but the professional should explain the reasons and avoid misleading threats or promises. Financial, family or institutional pressures may affect a choice even when no one intends harm. Ask whether the patient wants private discussion or support. Cultural preferences for family involvement can be respected while still determining whose decision is being made and whether participation is genuinely chosen.']],
['Refusal is also a decision',[
'An adult with relevant decision-making capacity can generally refuse recommended care, subject to applicable legal exceptions and circumstances. A refusal should prompt exploration of understanding, concerns and alternatives, not an automatic declaration of incapacity. The patient may value a burden or outcome differently from the clinician. Explain foreseeable consequences, offer reasonable alternatives and clarify what care remains available. Respectful disagreement can coexist with continued support.',
'Document the information discussed, the patient’s expressed reasons where relevant, assessment of capacity and the plan. A refusal form is not a substitute for this work. Avoid punitive language or implying that declining one intervention means the person has rejected all care. If the person’s condition changes, reassess the decision as appropriate. The aim is a valid informed choice, not obtaining a particular answer from the patient.',order('Respond to a treatment refusal',['Clarify the specific refusal and immediate risk','Assess decision-making abilities and communication barriers','Explain consequences and reasonable alternatives','Check understanding and voluntariness','Respect the valid decision and arrange ongoing care','Document and reassess if circumstances change']),{t:'w',kind:'case',title:'A refusal at the bedside',start:'a',nodes:{a:{text:'An adult patient with pneumonia refuses admission and wants to go home. She explains the risk of worsening infection in her own words and says she must care for her children. What should the clinician do first?',choices:[['Assess her decision-making capacity for this choice and explore her reasons','b','Correct. Her ability to explain the risk suggests understanding; her reasons may reveal solvable barriers.',true],['Declare her incompetent because she disagrees','b','Disagreement is not a test of capacity.',false]]},b:{text:'She has capacity and still declines admission. Which response best respects her choice?',choices:[['Explain consequences, offer alternatives such as oral antibiotics and close follow-up, and document the informed refusal','c','Correct. A valid refusal is respected while care continues in another form.',true],['Discharge her with no plan because she refused','c','Refusing one intervention does not mean refusing all care.',false]]},c:{text:'She leaves with antibiotics, return precautions and a follow-up visit in two days. The note records the information given, her capacity assessment and her reasons.',end:true}}}]],
['Surrogates and urgent decisions',[
'When an adult lacks capacity for the decision, identify an authorized surrogate under applicable law and any valid designation. The surrogate’s role is to represent the patient, not simply choose what the surrogate personally prefers. Substituted judgment uses the patient’s known values and preferences; a best-interests approach is used when those preferences cannot reasonably be determined. The precise hierarchy and authority vary by jurisdiction. A relative’s presence does not automatically establish authority for every decision.',
'Continue involving the patient to the extent possible, even if they cannot make the entire decision. Urgent circumstances may permit necessary treatment without prior consent when the patient cannot participate and an appropriate decision-maker is unavailable, but this is a limited exception, not a shortcut for routine convenience. Follow applicable law and institutional procedure, document urgency and efforts to identify preferences, and obtain ongoing authorization when possible. Minor consent rules also vary by age, service and jurisdiction; do not generalize one exception to all pediatric care.']],
['Apply the distinction between signature and choice',[
'For the patient who signed but cannot explain, pause when clinically feasible and investigate whether the problem is inadequate explanation, language, anxiety or impaired capacity. Provide support and repeat the discussion. A signature should not be used to silence a concern. For the patient who refuses after understanding the options and consequences, examine capacity and voluntariness fairly rather than using agreement as the test of competence.',
'A useful case answer specifies what information is missing. Does the patient appreciate that the risk applies personally? Can they compare options according to their values? Is there coercion? Is the decision urgent? These questions prevent the false binary that every signed form is valid and every refusal is irrational. The professional’s responsibility is to support an informed decision within the legal and ethical framework, then maintain appropriate care and documentation.',
'Consent is ongoing when an intervention or circumstances materially change. New information, a different procedure or a significant change in goals may require a renewed discussion. The team should not assume that earlier authorization covers an unrelated plan simply because the patient remains in the same facility. Clear boundaries protect both patient choice and reliable professional communication.']]
],takeaways:['A signature records consent but does not create understanding.','Capacity is decision-specific and can change.','A valid refusal is not evidence of incapacity.'],terms:[['Substituted judgment','A decision based on the patient’s known values and preferences.'],['Best interests','A patient-centered evaluation when preferences cannot reasonably be determined.']],mcqs:[q('A signed form alone establishes:','Documentation, not necessarily adequate informed consent','Complete understanding','Capacity for every decision','Permission for unrelated treatment','Consent is a process beyond the signature.'),q('Which ability is relevant to capacity?','Appreciating how information applies personally','Agreeing with the clinician','Having a particular diagnosis','Being free of every disability','Capacity concerns decision abilities.'),q('Refusal of recommended care automatically proves incapacity?','No, assess understanding and reasoning fairly','Yes whenever risk is serious','Only if family disagrees','Yes if a form is unsigned','Disagreement is not a capacity test.'),q('Implied permission for a routine measurement:','Does not authorize unrelated interventions','Covers every future procedure','Eliminates documentation','Overrides refusal','Consent has a defined scope.'),q('Substituted judgment relies on:','The patient’s known values','The surrogate’s convenience','The cheapest option only','The clinician’s personal preferences','The surrogate represents the patient.'),q('A communication barrier should lead first to:','Appropriate communication support','Automatic incapacity labeling','Ignoring the patient','Family control in every case','Support may enable a valid decision.'),q('Emergency consent exceptions are:','Limited by urgency, circumstances and applicable rules','Available whenever a form is inconvenient','A replacement for all consent','Identical for every setting','They are not routine shortcuts.'),q('A material change in the proposed intervention may require:','A renewed consent discussion','No further explanation','Automatic consent from the old form','Only a billing change','Authorization should match the actual plan.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare a patient who signs but cannot explain a procedure with a patient who understands it and refuses. Describe the professional response to each.',rubric:[['Distinguish form from process',['signature','understanding','process']],['Assess capacity and barriers',['capacity','communication','reason']],['Respect valid refusal',['refusal','voluntary','respect']],['Document and maintain care',['document','ongoing','alternative']]],model:'For the signed form, assess understanding and communication barriers, provide accessible explanation and pause when feasible until the consent process is adequate. A signature does not prove capacity or comprehension. For the informed refusal, assess decision-specific abilities and voluntariness without equating disagreement with incapacity. Explain consequences and alternatives, respect a valid decision within applicable law and continue appropriate care. Document the discussion, assessment and follow-up for both patients.'},sources:[RULES,CONSENT,CAPACITY]};

const L4={title:'Negligence, malpractice and professional liability',hook:'Two patients experience the same complication. In one case the team followed an appropriate process; in the other, a critical result was ignored. The outcome alone cannot establish negligence. Liability analysis examines duty, conduct, causation and harm.',objectives:['Explain the common elements of a negligence claim.','Distinguish an adverse outcome, error and legally established malpractice.','Recognize intentional tort concepts and vicarious liability.','Connect disclosure, documentation and system improvement without assuming universal legal protections.'],sections:[
['Start with the required elements',[
'Negligence commonly requires a duty, breach of that duty, causation and legally recognized harm. Duty identifies an obligation owed in the circumstances. Breach concerns conduct falling below the applicable standard. Causation links the breach to the injury, and damages concern the harm recognized by law. The precise formulation and proof requirements depend on jurisdiction. A professional malpractice claim applies these ideas to professional care; it is not simply a synonym for an unhappy patient or an unexpected outcome.',
'Use a case timeline to keep the elements separate. A duty to review a critical result may exist within an established care relationship. Failing to act could be a breach if it falls below the applicable standard. The analysis still asks whether that failure caused or contributed to a legally relevant injury. If the same outcome would have occurred despite appropriate care, causation may be contested. Conversely, a near miss may reveal serious safety problems even when compensable injury did not occur.']],
['The standard of care is contextual',[
'A standard of care concerns what an appropriately qualified professional should do under relevant circumstances. Expert evidence often helps establish and evaluate it, subject to legal rules. Guidelines, policies, training and accepted practice can be relevant evidence, but no single document necessarily decides every case. A guideline may allow clinical judgment, and a policy may be outdated or unsuitable for an unusual situation. Following a checklist does not excuse ignoring a clear change in the patient’s condition.',
'Distinguish what was knowable at the time from what became obvious afterward. Hindsight can make an uncertain choice appear inevitable. A fair review reconstructs the information available, the decisions made and the alternatives reasonably accessible. This does not mean every error is excused by uncertainty. It means the analysis should evaluate actual conduct in context rather than infer fault solely from the eventual result.',match('Match the negligence element',[['An obligation owed in the circumstances','Duty'],['Conduct below the applicable standard','Breach'],['Link between breach and injury','Causation'],['Legally recognized injury or loss','Damages']])]],
['Different wrongs require different concepts',[
'Intentional tort concepts include battery, assault, false imprisonment and certain privacy or reputation harms, with legal definitions varying by jurisdiction. In a healthcare context, unauthorized touching can raise a battery issue; threatening conduct can raise an assault issue under the applicable elements. False imprisonment concerns unlawful restraint of freedom. Do not assume that any touching, disagreement or delayed discharge automatically satisfies these legal definitions. Facts, authority and consent matter.',
'Contract claims concern enforceable agreements and alleged failures to meet their terms. Express agreements are stated, while implied agreements may arise from conduct under applicable law. A professional should avoid guaranteeing an outcome that cannot be assured. Vicarious liability can make an organization responsible for certain acts of its employees or agents within the relevant relationship and scope. It does not mean an employer is automatically responsible for every act a worker performs anywhere.',{t:'w',kind:'sort',title:'Intentional tort, negligence or contract?',buckets:['Intentional tort','Negligence','Contract issue'],items:[['Performing a procedure the patient explicitly refused (battery)',0],['Threatening a patient with an injection to force compliance (assault)',0],['Restraining a capable patient without legal authority (false imprisonment)',0],['Failing to follow up a critical lab result that harms the patient',1],['Leaving a sponge inside a surgical patient',1],['Promising a guaranteed cure that does not occur',2]]}]],
['Respond to an incident without making it worse',[
'The first priority is appropriate patient care and prompt communication to the responsible clinical team. Preserve factual records and relevant evidence according to policy. Document observations, actions and notifications accurately and promptly; do not alter an earlier entry to make the timeline look better. Corrections should preserve an audit trail and follow the record system’s procedures. Speculation about blame belongs neither in a fabricated clinical note nor in casual discussion with unrelated people.',
'Disclosure should be truthful and handled by the appropriate team, including what is known, what is being addressed and what remains uncertain. Do not speculate beyond the facts or promise an outcome. Laws concerning apologies, reporting and protected review materials differ, so do not assume every statement or incident report has the same legal protection. Risk management and legal counsel can guide procedure, but their involvement should not replace the obligation to care for and communicate with the patient.',order('Respond to a suspected care-related harm',['Address the patient’s immediate needs','Notify responsible clinical personnel','Preserve and document factual information','Follow the appropriate disclosure and reporting process','Investigate contributing factors','Implement and evaluate corrective actions'])]],
['Professional and organizational responsibility',[
'An individual error may occur within a system that made failure more likely: unclear ownership, fatigue, similar packaging, inaccessible results or excessive alerts. Safety analysis examines these conditions while maintaining appropriate accountability. A system explanation is not a declaration that no individual responsibility exists. Equally, identifying one person’s mistake does not show that the organization has corrected the conditions that will expose the next person to the same risk.',
'Professional liability insurance, institutional policies and legal representation have specific terms and limits. Students should understand their existence without assuming a policy protects every act or removes duties. Time limits for claims, rules about expert testimony and damages vary by jurisdiction. In an educational vignette without these facts, state the general framework and identify the missing legal questions rather than inventing an exact filing deadline or award.']],
['Compare the two complication cases',[
'For the patient who experienced a recognized complication despite appropriate assessment, consent and management, the poor result alone does not demonstrate breach. For the ignored result, determine who had responsibility, what the result required, whether it was received and what action occurred. Then examine whether delay caused harm. A malfunctioning notification system may be relevant to both individual and organizational analysis. The clinical response and safety investigation should proceed even while legal liability remains unresolved.',
'A strong written answer separates “this warrants investigation” from “malpractice has been proven.” It names the elements, applies known facts and avoids filling gaps with assumptions. It also proposes prevention: define result ownership, acknowledgment, backup review and escalation. Measure whether critical results reach a responsible professional and lead to documented action within the appropriate interval. This connects legal reasoning with a practical safety response.',
'The distinction between a near miss and a harmless process is especially important. A patient may avoid injury because another professional notices the problem or because circumstances happen to be favorable. That fortunate outcome does not validate the original process. Reporting and learning from near misses can prevent future injury, while the legal analysis still recognizes that harm and causation have separate roles in a damages claim.']]
],takeaways:['A poor outcome does not alone establish malpractice.','Analyze duty, breach, causation and harm separately.','Truthful records and system improvement remain essential while liability is assessed.'],terms:[['Breach','Failure to meet an applicable duty or standard.'],['Vicarious liability','Responsibility for another’s acts under a legally recognized relationship.']],mcqs:[q('Which is not established solely by a poor outcome?','Breach of the standard of care','That an outcome occurred','That review may be useful','That the patient may need support','Outcome alone does not establish negligent conduct.'),q('The link between a breach and injury is:','Causation','Certification','Consent form storage','Credentialing','Causation connects conduct with harm.'),q('A near miss can:','Reveal serious process risk without resulting injury','Prove the process is safe','Always establish damages','Never require review','Avoided harm can still expose a failure mode.'),q('A correction to a medical record should:','Preserve an appropriate audit trail','Secretly erase the original','Be backdated to conceal delay','State guesses as facts','Transparent corrections protect record integrity.'),q('Vicarious liability depends on:','The legally relevant relationship and scope','Any employment connection anywhere','Only the worker’s age','Only a verbal complaint','Its application is contextual.'),q('A guideline is:','Potential evidence, not necessarily the sole answer in every case','Always a complete legal defense','Never relevant','A guarantee of outcome','Standards require contextual evaluation.'),q('The first response to suspected patient harm is:','Address immediate care needs','Rewrite the chart','Debate blame publicly','Destroy duplicate records','Patient care comes first.'),q('Which phrasing is most defensible with incomplete facts?','The incident warrants investigation of negligence elements','Malpractice is proven by the complication','No liability is possible because a policy exists','An apology always admits liability everywhere','The conclusion should match the evidence.')],frq:{prompt:'A critical result was not acted on and a patient deteriorated. Apply the negligence framework and propose immediate and preventive actions without assuming liability is already proven.',rubric:[['Apply duty and breach',['duty','responsibility','standard']],['Separate causation and harm',['causation','injury','harm']],['Care and truthful documentation',['care','document','preserve']],['Investigate system and prevention',['notification','ownership','escalation']]],model:'Identify who owed the review duty and whether the response fell below the applicable standard, then separately evaluate whether the delay caused the patient’s injury. Deterioration alone does not prove every element. Address immediate clinical needs, notify responsible personnel and preserve accurate records. Follow appropriate disclosure and reporting procedures. Investigate result routing, acknowledgment, workload and backup coverage, then implement clear ownership and escalation with monitoring for timely action.'},sources:[RULES,COURTS,CONSENT]};

const L1={title:'Law, professional ethics and bioethical reasoning',hook:'A family asks a clinician to hide a diagnosis from an adult patient. The family wants to protect the patient from distress; the patient has asked for direct information. A good response must distinguish facts, legal requirements, professional duties and competing values.',objectives:['Distinguish law, ethics, professional codes and organizational policy.','Apply autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice, fidelity and veracity.','Compare consequences, duties and character in ethical reasoning.','Build a justified response that acknowledges uncertainty and stakeholder interests.'],sections:[
['Different sources of obligation',[
'Law consists of enforceable rules established through recognized governmental authority. Ethics examines what ought to be done and why. Professional codes articulate responsibilities associated with a role, while organizational policies describe how a particular institution expects work to be performed. These sources overlap but are not interchangeable. A policy may be stricter than a legal minimum, and an action may be legally permitted while still raising a serious ethical concern. Conversely, sincere moral conviction does not automatically exempt someone from an applicable legal duty.',
'Bioethics applies ethical reasoning to health, life sciences and related technologies. It addresses bedside decisions, research, public health and resource allocation. Medical law in this course uses United States examples, but state rules and factual circumstances can change the legal answer. A reliable case analysis identifies the jurisdiction, professional role and setting before applying a rule. Distinguish a general educational principle from a claim that a particular action is lawful in every place.']],
['Principles reveal the tension',[
'Autonomy concerns a person’s ability to make informed, voluntary choices about their own life and care. Beneficence supports actions intended to benefit the patient, and nonmaleficence requires attention to avoiding or reducing harm. Justice concerns fair treatment and distribution of opportunities, resources and burdens. Fidelity involves keeping commitments and sustaining trust; veracity concerns truthfulness. These principles guide questions rather than function as a calculator that produces one answer whenever words are added together.',
'In the opening case, the family’s protective intention reflects concern for benefit and harm, while the patient’s request raises autonomy and truthfulness. The clinician should clarify what the patient wants to know and how they prefer information to be shared, rather than assume that every patient wants either complete immediate disclosure or family control. Family involvement can support autonomy when it follows the patient’s preferences. It undermines autonomy when the family substitutes its own preference without an appropriate basis.',match('Match the ethical concern',[['Respect an informed voluntary choice','Autonomy'],['Promote a worthwhile benefit','Beneficence'],['Reduce foreseeable harm','Nonmaleficence'],['Distribute burdens and benefits fairly','Justice'],['Communicate truthfully','Veracity']])]],
['Different ethical lenses',[
'A consequentialist approach evaluates outcomes, asking which action produces the most defensible balance of benefits and harms. A duty-based approach emphasizes obligations and limits that should not simply be traded away for convenience. Virtue ethics considers the character and practical judgment of a good professional, including honesty, courage and humility. Care-oriented reasoning emphasizes relationships, dependence and responsiveness to particular people. These lenses can illuminate different parts of the same case.',
'A purely numerical outcome comparison may overlook rights or unequal burdens. A rigid rule applied without context may ignore the purpose of the duty or an important exception. Character language alone can become vague unless connected to observable actions. A strong answer therefore explains its reasoning: what benefit is expected, whose interests are protected, which duties constrain the choice and how the response respects the people involved. Naming a principle without applying it is weaker than explaining the actual conflict.',{t:'w',kind:'ethicslab',title:'Work through three short ethics cases'}]],
['Separate facts, assumptions and preferences',[
'Start with clinically and legally relevant facts: who the patient is, what decision is needed, how urgent it is, whether the patient can participate and what information is known. Identify uncertainty explicitly. A family’s prediction that disclosure will destroy hope is a concern to explore, not an established fact. A clinician’s assumption that the patient shares the clinician’s values is also not evidence. Ask what additional information could change the decision.',
'Identify stakeholders without giving every stakeholder identical decision authority. The patient, family, clinicians, institution and community may all be affected, but their roles differ. Distinguish the patient’s expressed preference from a relative’s interpretation and from an institutional convenience. Generate realistic options, including changes in timing, communication support or consultation. Ethical analysis often improves when a false choice between two extremes is replaced with a carefully designed third option.',order('Build a defensible case analysis',['Clarify the facts and immediate safety needs','Identify the decision-maker and stakeholder interests','Check applicable duties, rules and uncertainty','Compare realistic options and consequences','Choose and explain a proportionate response','Document, communicate and review'])]],
['Consultation supports rather than replaces responsibility',[
'An ethics consultation can help clarify values, facilitate discussion and identify options. Legal counsel or compliance personnel address legal interpretation and institutional obligations. A supervisor can clarify role and procedure. These resources are complementary. An ethics committee does not automatically authorize an unlawful action, and a legal opinion does not settle every moral concern. Consultation should be timely and should not become a way to delay an urgent decision without a safety plan.',
'Documentation should describe the issue, relevant facts, participants, preferences, options considered and reasoning behind the decision. Avoid judgmental labels such as “difficult family” when a concrete description would be more useful. Record uncertainty and follow-up responsibilities. If new facts emerge, revisit the plan. Ethical consistency means applying a defensible process to changing circumstances, not refusing to reconsider an earlier conclusion.']],
['Resolve the disclosure request respectfully',[
'The clinician can acknowledge the family’s protective concern, ask what harm they fear and explain the importance of learning the patient’s preferences. Speak with the patient in an appropriate setting, using communication support if needed, and ask how they wish to receive information and involve family. If the patient wants direct information and can make the decision, the plan should respect that preference while delivering information sensitively and offering support. Avoid promising the family secrecy that conflicts with the patient’s expressed wishes.',
'This response combines truthfulness with compassion. It does not assume that honesty requires overwhelming detail at once or that kindness requires deception. The team can pace explanations, check understanding and arrange emotional support. In an exam response, explicitly connect each action to the concern it addresses. That makes the reasoning reviewable and shows that the answer is more than a slogan about autonomy.',
'Finally, distinguish moral distress from an ethical disagreement. Moral distress can arise when someone believes they know the appropriate action but feels constrained from taking it. The response may require organizational support, clarification of authority or a safer route for raising concerns. A disagreement, by contrast, may reflect different reasonable interpretations of values or facts. Both deserve respectful attention, but they may require different remedies.']]
],takeaways:['Law, policy and ethics overlap without being identical.','Apply principles to facts rather than merely naming them.','A defensible response explains authority, options, consequences and follow-up.'],terms:[['Veracity','Truthfulness in communication.'],['Moral distress','Distress associated with perceived inability to act on an ethical judgment.']],mcqs:[q('Which statement best distinguishes law and ethics?','Legal permission does not resolve every ethical concern','Every ethical preference is legally enforceable','Policy always overrides law','Ethics applies only to research','They overlap but answer different questions.'),q('Respect for an informed voluntary choice most directly concerns:','Autonomy','Efficiency','Inventory control','Punishment','Autonomy addresses self-determination.'),q('A family’s prediction that disclosure will destroy hope should initially be treated as:','A concern to explore rather than an established fact','A binding legal order','Proof of incapacity','Permission to deceive','Predictions require clarification and evidence.'),q('Duty-based reasoning emphasizes:','Obligations and limits on action','Only aggregate outcomes','Only personal popularity','Only financial cost','Duties may constrain choices even when convenient alternatives exist.'),q('An ethics consultation primarily helps:','Clarify values, communication and options','Automatically override law','Replace all clinical judgment','Guarantee unanimity','Consultation supports reasoned decisions.'),q('Which documentation is most useful?','Specific concerns, preferences and reasoning','A label such as difficult family','Only the final signature','An unsupported assumption about motives','Concrete records make the process understandable.'),q('Family involvement supports autonomy when it:','Reflects the patient’s preferences','Always replaces the patient','Requires withholding all information','Ignores the patient’s wishes','The patient may choose relational decision support.'),q('A strong ethics response should:','Connect proposed actions to identified concerns','List principles without application','Ignore uncertainty','Assume only two extreme options exist','Reasoning must show why an action addresses the case.')],frq:{prompt:'A family asks that a diagnosis be hidden from an adult patient who has requested direct information. Analyze the conflict and propose a respectful response.',rubric:[['Identify competing principles',['autonomy','truth','harm']],['Clarify patient preferences and authority',['patient','preference','capacity']],['Offer sensitive communication and support',['communicate','support','understand']],['Document and consult appropriately',['document','consult','reason']]],model:'The family’s concern reflects an intention to avoid harm, while the patient’s request engages autonomy and truthfulness. Clarify the feared harm and ask the patient how they want information and family involvement handled. If the patient can decide and wants direct information, respect that preference with sensitive pacing, clear explanation and support. Do not promise incompatible secrecy. Document the preferences and reasoning, and use ethics or legal consultation if a material uncertainty remains.'},sources:[RULES,CONSENT,BELMONT]};

const L2={title:'The legal system, professional scope and public duties',hook:'A hospital policy permits a task, but a trainee has not been authorized or trained to perform it. A second case involves a report required by state law. Knowing who made a rule and whom it applies to is essential before deciding what to do.',objectives:['Distinguish statutes, regulations, judicial decisions and policy.','Compare civil, criminal and administrative proceedings.','Explain licensure, certification, credentialing and scope.','Analyze reporting and emergency duties without assuming uniform state rules.'],sections:[
['Identify the source and authority of a rule',[
'A statute is enacted by a legislature. A regulation is issued by an authorized administrative agency to implement law within its authority. Courts interpret and apply law in disputes, and judicial decisions can establish precedent within the relevant legal system. Organizational policies guide local practice but do not erase applicable statutes or regulations. A professional code can influence standards and discipline without being identical to a statute. Always ask what kind of source is being cited.',
'The United States has federal and state legal systems with different areas of authority and sometimes overlapping requirements. Federal courts have defined jurisdiction; they do not hear every disagreement merely because it involves healthcare. Many professional licensing and tort questions depend heavily on state law. A current agency guidance page can help explain a requirement, but the underlying law, effective dates and relevant court decisions may matter when a disputed question needs formal interpretation.']],
['Different proceedings address different wrongs',[
'Civil proceedings commonly resolve disputes and may provide compensation or other remedies. Criminal proceedings involve government prosecution for alleged offenses and can impose criminal penalties. Administrative proceedings can address licensing, regulatory compliance or benefits under an agency’s authority. One event can raise more than one type of issue. A serious medication incident could lead to an internal review, a civil claim and professional licensing scrutiny, depending on the facts.',
'The parties, procedures and standards of proof differ. In a typical civil negligence case, the plaintiff must establish the required elements under the applicable standard; a poor outcome alone is not a complete case. Criminal guilt must be established beyond a reasonable doubt. Do not use that criminal standard as the default for every administrative or civil question. Appeals generally review alleged legal errors rather than simply repeating a trial because one party dislikes the result.',match('Identify the source or process',[['Legislature enacts a requirement','Statute'],['Authorized agency issues implementing rules','Regulation'],['A court interprets law in a dispute','Judicial decision'],['A board reviews a professional license','Administrative process']]),{t:'w',kind:'sort',title:'Which kind of proceeding?',buckets:['Civil','Criminal','Administrative'],items:[['A patient sues for compensation after an injury',0],['A family files a wrongful-death claim',0],['The state prosecutes insurance fraud',1],['A clinician is charged with diverting controlled substances',1],['A nursing board suspends a license',2],['A state agency cites a facility after an inspection',2]]}]],
['Professional permission has several layers',[
'Licensure is governmental permission to practice a regulated profession under defined conditions. Certification generally recognizes that specified standards have been met, often through a professional organization; it does not automatically confer legal permission to perform every task. Credentialing verifies qualifications, and privileging authorizes particular activities within an institution. Scope of practice concerns what a professional may do under applicable law and standards. These concepts overlap but should not be collapsed into one credential.',
'Competence also matters. A task may fall within a profession’s general scope but still require individual training, supervision or demonstrated skill. A trainee’s assignment must fit educational and institutional requirements as well as applicable law. An order from a supervisor does not make an unauthorized task lawful or safe. The appropriate response is to clarify the assignment, explain the limitation and obtain qualified support through the designated chain rather than silently proceeding or abandoning the patient.']],
['Delegation and documentation',[
'Delegation assigns a task within an authorized relationship, with attention to the task, person, circumstances, direction and supervision. The exact legal allocation of responsibility varies by profession and jurisdiction. The delegating professional must evaluate suitability, and the recipient must recognize limits and report problems. A task’s routine appearance does not eliminate its risk. The need for clinical judgment, changing patient status or specialized assessment may make delegation inappropriate in a particular case.',
'Document relevant instructions, observations and escalation without manufacturing certainty. If a task cannot be completed safely, communicate promptly and identify the needed assistance. Preserve continuity while the issue is resolved. A written policy is useful evidence of local expectations, but merely following it does not guarantee compliance if the policy conflicts with law or the circumstances require further professional judgment. Institutions should review policies when rules or practice conditions change.',order('Respond to an uncertain task assignment',['Identify the task and immediate patient need','Check role, scope, training and authorization','Explain the limitation and seek clarification','Arrange qualified support and appropriate supervision','Document the action and follow-up'])]],
['Public duty and reporting',[
'Healthcare professionals may have reporting duties related to communicable disease, suspected abuse or other defined circumstances. The trigger, recipient, timing and protected information depend on the applicable law and role. Suspicion thresholds can differ from proof standards used in court, so a professional should not assume that a report must wait until they personally establish guilt. Equally, reporting should follow the proper channel rather than becoming public accusation or unrestricted sharing.',
'Confidentiality often has legally defined exceptions or permissions for public purposes. The existence of a reporting duty does not make the entire record public. Identify the information required, authorized recipient and documentation process, and consult designated resources when uncertain. Some duties involve immediate safety and cannot wait for a routine meeting. A student should learn the local policy and supervisory route rather than memorize one state’s rule as a universal national rule.']],
['Emergency obligations and practical reasoning',[
'EMTALA is a United States federal framework that applies to covered hospitals and requires an appropriate medical screening examination when its conditions are met, and stabilizing treatment or an appropriate transfer for an emergency medical condition within the law’s requirements. It is not a general promise that all healthcare is free, nor a rule that every office must provide every service. Scope, setting and circumstances are essential to an accurate explanation. Payment concerns must not be used to improperly delay covered emergency evaluation.',
'Return to the trainee. First ensure the patient’s immediate needs are addressed by qualified personnel, then clarify whether the task is within role, training and authorization. For the reporting case, identify the jurisdiction, professional role, suspected condition and proper recipient. These cases share a method: establish facts, identify controlling authority, check scope and act through an accountable process. The answer is neither blind obedience to any instruction nor refusal whenever uncertainty exists.',
'A useful study exercise is to annotate a scenario with the source of each obligation. Mark one statement as a law, another as a local policy, another as an ethical duty and another as an unsupported assumption. This reveals when an answer uses the right principle at the wrong level. It also helps explain why a policy manual, professional textbook and legal database can provide different kinds of information without necessarily contradicting one another.']]
],takeaways:['Source, jurisdiction and role determine a rule’s application.','Licensure, certification, credentialing and competence are distinct.','Public duties require correct channels and proportionate information handling.'],terms:[['Licensure','Governmental authorization to practice a regulated profession.'],['Jurisdiction','Legal authority to hear or govern a matter.']],mcqs:[q('A rule enacted by a legislature is a:','Statute','Hospital memo','Clinical hypothesis','Credential','Statutes come from legislatures.'),q('A licensing-board action is generally a type of:','Administrative proceeding','Randomized trial','Private negotiation only','Criminal conviction automatically','Boards exercise regulatory authority.'),q('Certification always authorizes every professional task?','No, legal scope and other permissions still matter','Yes in every state','Only if the certificate is printed','Yes if an employer requests it','Certification is not unlimited practice authority.'),q('A supervisor requests an unfamiliar unauthorized task. Best response?','Clarify limits and arrange qualified support','Proceed because an order removes responsibility','Leave without communicating','Falsely document completion','Patient continuity and scope both matter.'),q('Beyond a reasonable doubt is associated with:','Criminal proof','Every civil claim','Every incident report','Every quality audit','Criminal prosecution has a distinct proof standard.'),q('A reporting duty means the full chart becomes public?','No, disclose through the authorized process','Yes automatically','Only if social media is used','Yes whenever suspicion exists','Purpose and recipient remain constrained.'),q('EMTALA is best understood as:','A scoped emergency-care obligation for covered hospitals','A guarantee that all care is free','A universal rule for every private office','A replacement for all state law','The law has defined settings and requirements.'),q('A policy that conflicts with applicable law:','Does not erase the legal requirement','Always overrides it','Creates a new license','Makes review unnecessary','Local policy cannot simply cancel controlling law.')],frq:{prompt:'A trainee is asked to perform a task allowed by a hospital policy but outside their training and authorization. Explain the relevant sources of authority and a safe response.',rubric:[['Distinguish policy and controlling authority',['policy','law','scope']],['Assess competence and authorization',['training','competence','authorization']],['Maintain care through escalation',['qualified','supervisor','support']],['Document and clarify',['document','clarify','follow']]],model:'A hospital policy describes local expectations but does not replace applicable scope rules, trainee authorization or competence requirements. Clarify the task and immediate patient need, explain the training and authorization limits, and seek qualified personnel or appropriate supervision through the designated chain. Do not perform the task merely because someone requested it, and do not abandon the patient’s needs. Document relevant communication and follow-up, and resolve any policy inconsistency with the responsible institutional resources.'},sources:[RULES,COURTS,EMTALA]};

